      "title": "Benchmarking machine learning and molecular dynamics methods for the prediction of the thermophysical properties of organic liquids",
      "publish_date": "2026-09-29",
      "excerpts": [
        "Benchmarking machine learning and molecular dynamics methods for the prediction of the thermophysical properties of organic liquids\n* Authors: Egheosa Ogbomo, Fionn Carman, Daniele Dini, James P. Ewen\n* Journal: Digital Discovery\n* Published: 2026-09-29\n* DOI: 10.1039/d6dd00260a\n* PMID: 42852142\n* PMCID: PMC13647254\nAbstract\nEquilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations of eight representative molecules reproduce density accurately but show systematic, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for the viscosity and thermal conductivity.\nAll the MD simulations were performed using the LAMMPS software.^102 EMD simulations were employed to calculate the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of the eight target molecules at 40 °C and 100 °C, consistent with conditions used to determine lubricant viscosity index values.^8 All simulations were performed using the LOPLS-AA force\nDensity, viscosity, and thermal conductivity were computed at 40 and 100 °C with the LOPLS-AA force field,^103 assigned directly from each molecule's SMILES string using our open-source builder tool. The same properties were predicted with the best-performing XGBoost descriptor models identified in the ML benchmarking above.\nThis agreement is expected, since liquid densities are governed largely by the repulsive and dispersion parameters and force fields such as OPLS are often directly fitted to reproduce liquid densities.103,104 The XGBoost density predictions carry errors of similar magnitude but opposite character.\nDespite these limitations, the force field largely preserves the ordering of the reference viscosity values for the eight fluids at both temperatures, suggesting that MD simulations remain useful for relative screening, even where its absolute viscosity predictions require caution.\nS14b) reaches an early plateau from which the conductivity is read before noise accumulates at longer times; unlike the viscosity, the estimates are already converged within the first 1–2 ns of trajectory (Fig. S15). LOPLS over-predicts κ for every molecule at both temperatures, with deviations ranging from +7 to +96% (Fig. 7c and S10c).\nThe two polyethers are the clearest exceptions, their thermal conductivity is over-predicted by less than their aliphatic hydrogen content alone would imply. This ordering is consistent with a vibrational contribution. Hydrocarbons contain C–H bonds that produce very fast, high-frequency intramolecular stretching and bending vibrations.\nUnlike the viscosity, the LOPLS force field does not reproduce even the relative ordering of the thermal conductivities of the eight fluids. The OOF XGBoost values for this set stay within 8% of the reference values at both temperatures and preserve the ordering.\nFig."
