      "excerpts": [
        "0.5 kcal/mol, respectively; significant gains were made for relative heats of vaporization of isomeric series. Results for self-diffusion coefficients also reproduce well experimental data for linear alkane liquids up to hexadecane.\nDeficiencies in the ordering and range of computed heats of vaporization for isomers are also noted and improved upon. The new hydrocarbon parameters presented here are part of the OPLS/2020 force field, which has also addressed treatment of cation-π interactions in a formally non-polarizable force field.\nIn setup 2, denoted by MD-PME, electrostatic interactions beyond the cutoff radius were also included using the Particle Mesh Ewald (PME) algorithm 27 with an interpolation order of 6, a relative tolerance of 10 −6 , and a Fourier spacing of 1.2 Å.\nButane is also treated uniquely in the CHARMM C27r force field. 10 These changes are needed to bring the force field results into close agreement with best current estimates for linear alkane conformers.\nRelative conformational energies for alkanes obtained by performing energy minimizations in the gas phase with the OPLS/2020 and OPLS-AA force fields are given in Table 3 . The main source of reference data for conformational energetics is results of high-end quantum-mechanical calculations.\nBoth force fields also give a small range, 0.02 g/cm 3 , for the hexanes, while only OPLS/2020 gives the correct density order for pentane and neopentane, and for butane and isobutane (2-methylpropane).\nFor cyclic alkanes versus linear ones with the same number of carbon atoms, the force fields both reproduce the pattern of higher densities and heats of vaporization for the cyclic molecules.\n58 , 59 The complexity increases when the simulations are conducted in the NPT ensemble, because barostats also alter the dynamics of the system in order to keep the pressure at a desired value. The implications of such thermostat- and barostat-dependent dynamical artifacts for computer simulations are beyond the scope of this work.\nThis indicates that liquid hexadecane does not undergo transitions to the gel or the crystalline phase above its melting point (18 °C) in simulations with OPLS/2020, and that the calculated values for the diffusion coefficients are sufficiently converged."
